DESCRIPTION:[DDPS seminar] Invariant Manifold-Based Nonlinear Model Redu
 ction for Fluid Dynamics\n\n\nWhen: Thursday\, January 22\, 2026\, 11:00
  AM-12:00 PM (UTC-08:00) Pacific Time (US & Canada)\n\nWhere: https://ll
 nlfed.webex.com/llnlfed/j.php?MTID=m75f0ed0d49768efdfffdd2f650b135da\n\n
 \nWe will host Bálint Kaszás (Stanford University\, https://github.com/b
 alintkaszas) as a speaker in the DDPS seminar.\n\n\n\nInvariant Manifold
 -Based Nonlinear Model Reduction for Fluid Dynamics\n\n\n\nBálint Kaszás
 \n\n\n\nStanford University\n\n\n\nThursday\, January 22\, 2026\n\n11:00
  AM\n\nWebEx\n\n\nAbstract: This talk presents a data-driven\, dynamics-
 based approach to model reduction of nonlinear systems\, with a focus on
  fluid flows. Recent advances in the theory of dynamical systems make it
  possible to restrict high-dimensional dynamics to very low-dimensional 
 invariant manifolds\, called spectral submanifolds (SSMs). These manifol
 ds are the nonlinear continuations of spectral subspaces of the lineariz
 ed system at stationary states and act as low-dimensional attractors. We
  show that such manifolds capture the essential dynamics\, from transiti
 ons between coexisting steady states to chaotic dynamics\, and enable th
 e construction of predictive reduced-order models. We illustrate invaria
 nt manifold-based modeling on canonical wall-bounded shear flows and on 
 experimental measurements of a fluid-structure interaction problem.\n\n\
 nBio: Bálint Kaszás is a CTR Postdoctoral Fellow at the Center for Turbu
 lence Research at Stanford University. Prior to joining CTR\, he was a p
 ostdoctoral researcher at ETH Zurich. He received his MSc in Physics in 
 Hungary and his Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering from ETH Zurich. His cur
 rent research interests include geophysical fluid dynamics\, Lagrangian 
 transport phenomena\, and dynamical systems theory.\n\n📚 DDPS seminar i
 s organized by libROM team ( www.librom.net<https://nam12.safelinks.prot
 ection.outlook.com/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.librom.net%2F&data=05%7C02%7Crz
