 IkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=tiynzgiISHCU053QlYgs
 wi%2B2E%2BMWM1B7nn7U3hLcOVk%3D&reserved=0>)\n\n\nABSTRACT\n\nThe Vlasov–
 Poisson system is a cornerstone in modeling collisionless plasmas\, capt
 uring how self-consistent electric fields evolve from particle dynamics.
  However\, its infinite-dimensional structure poses significant challeng
 es for simulation—especially when accuracy\, efficiency\, and fidelity t
 o physical laws are crucial. Traditional Particle-in-Cell (PIC) methods\
 , while widely used\, often require large numbers of particles and suffe
 r from noise and poor resolution in sparsely sampled regions of phase sp
 ace.\n\nIn this talk\, I present a moment-enhanced PIC method that enric
 hes each simulation particle with local moment data—such as density\, mo
 mentum\, and higher-order derivatives. This added structure enables a mo
 re accurate representation of the distribution function with fewer parti
 cles. The approach is grounded in a Poisson bracket formulation inspired
  by Scovel–Weinstein reduction\, leading to a finite-dimensional model t
 hat retains the Hamiltonian structure of the original Vlasov dynamics.\n
 \nNumerical results for the 1D Vlasov–Poisson system show improved stabi
 lity\, reduced noise\, and better conservation of physical invariants su
 ch as energy and momentum. This framework paves the way for efficient\, 
 structure-preserving kinetic solvers in plasma physics and beyond.\n\n\n
