BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTENDEE;CN="Choi, Youngsoo";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="choi15@llnl.gov";P
 ARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;ROLE=CHAIR:mailto:choi15@llnl.gov
ATTENDEE;CN="Data Driven Physics Simulation";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="dd
 ps@llnl.gov";ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT:mailto:ddps@llnl.gov
ATTENDEE;CN="Kang, Sung Ha";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="kang@math.gatech.ed
 u";ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT:mailto:kang@math.gatech.edu
CREATED:20260125T225041Z
DESCRIPTION:We will host Sung Ha Kang (Georgia Institute of Technology\,
  https://kang.math.gatech.edu/) as a DDPS seminar speaker. Hope to see y
 ou many of you at the seminar:\n\n\nIdentifying differential equations f
 rom single observation with numerical methods: IDENT to WeakIDENT and mo
 re\n\nSung Ha Kang\n\nGeorgia Institute of Technology\n\nFriday\, April 
 18\, 2025\n10:00 AM\nWebEx\n\n\nAbstract: We explore identifying underla
 ying differential equation from given single set of noisy time dependent
  data.    We assume that the governing equation is a linear combination 
 of linear and nonlinear differential term\, and the identification can b
 e formulated as a linear system\, with the feature matrix multiplied by 
 a coefficient vector.  This talk will cover a collection of our recent w
 ork on this topic: starting from numerical time evolution (IDENT)\,  we 
 consider robust identification methods using successively denoised diffe
 rentiation and subspace pursuit\, and using weak form for ODE and PDE re
 covery.  Using weak form shows robustness against higher level of noise 
 and higher order derivative in underlying equation.   We further conside
 r Group subspace pursuit for varying coefficient cases and using Fourier
  domain for identification.\n\nBio: Sung Ha Kang is a distinguished Prof
 essor of Mathematics at the Georgia Institute of Technology\, where she 
 has taught since August 2017. Previously\, she held positions as Associa
 te Professor and Assistant Professor in the School of Mathematics at Geo
 rgia Tech (2008–2017) and began her tenure-track career at the Universit
 y of Kentucky (2002–2008). She received her Ph.D. in Applied Mathematics
  from UCLA in 2002. Her research focuses on advanced mathematical approa
 ches to image processing\, using variational\, functional\, and PDE-base
 d methods for tasks like denoising\, deblurring\, inpainting\, and segme
 ntation. She also explores numerical methods and scientific computing\, 
 including data clustering and differential equation identification from 
 limited data. In addition to her research\, Dr. Kang serves as the CSE c
 oordinator for the School of Mathematics\, overseeing both the CSE Ph.D.
  and MS programs and mentoring students interested in applying mathemati
 cal ideas to practical challenges.\n\n📚 DDPS seminar is organized by li
 bROM team ( www.librom.net<http://www.librom.net> ).\n🗓️ DDPS seminar s
 chedule: https://www.librom.net/ddps.html<https://www.youtube.com/redire
 ct?event=video_description&redir_token=QUFFLUhqblRkS256NDFhdjRyNHpRRFYwS
 m5jT0t0MVNvZ3xBQ3Jtc0treERKS1BuSkhPd3FHaXFqRDZPUnZtRjh1b01WcjJuczBSZFA1a
