ATTENDEE;CN="Alessandro Alla";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="alessandro.alla@u
 niroma1.it";ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT:mailto:alessandro.alla@uniroma1.it
ATTENDEE;CN="Choi, Youngsoo";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="choi15@llnl.gov";P
 ARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;ROLE=CHAIR:mailto:choi15@llnl.gov
ATTENDEE;CN="Data Driven Physics Simulation";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="dd
 ps@llnl.gov";ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT:mailto:ddps@llnl.gov
CREATED:20260125T225043Z
DESCRIPTION: We will host Alessandro Alla (Sapienza University of Rome\,
  http://www.alessandroalla.com/) as a DDPS seminar speaker. Hope to see 
 you many of you at the seminar:\n\n\nData-Driven Algorithms for Online I
 dentification and Control of Partial Differential Equations\n\nAlessandr
 o Alla\n\nSapienza University of Rome\n\nThursday\, June 5\, 2025\n10:00
  AM\nWebEx\n\nAbstract: We propose online algorithms to identify and con
 trol Partial Differential Equations (PDEs) with unknown parameters\, add
 ressing key challenges in the control of complex dynamical systems. We c
 onsider two scenarios. In the first\, the PDE is assumed observable give
 n a control input and an initial condition. Using an initial parameter e
 stimate\, we compute the control via the State-Dependent Riccati Equatio
 n (SDRE). The observed trajectory is then used to iteratively update the
  parameter configuration via Bayesian Linear Regression until a stopping
  criterion is met. The second scenario addresses cases with incomplete i
 nformation\, such as unknown initial conditions\, sparse boundary data a
 nd coefficient in the PDE. We leverage Physics-Informed Neural Networks 
 (PINNs) for open-loop optimal control problems\, deriving optimality con
 ditions via Lagrangian multipliers. Neural networks predict the state\, 
 adjoint\, and control variables\, while identifying parameters online ba
 sed on sparse data obtained from the uncontrolled problem. Numerical exa
 mples demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed methods\, even in hi
 ghly challenging scenarios characterized by significant uncertainty and 
 incomplete information. These are joint works with Giulia Bertaglia (Uni
 versity of Ferrara\, Italy)\, Elisa Calzola (University of Verona\, Ital
 y)\, and Agnese Pacifico (Sapienza\, University of Rome\, Italy).\n\nBio
 : Prof. Alla is an Associate Professor at Sapienza University of Rome (I
 taly) since 2024. He earned a Ph.D. in Applied Mathematics from Sapienza
  in 2014. Before joining Sapienza\, he held positions as an Assistant Pr
 ofessor at Ca' Foscari University of Venice (Italy)\, an Adjunct Profess
 or at PUC-Rio (Brazil)\, and a Postdoctoral Fellow at Florida State Univ
 ersity (USA) and the University of Hamburg (Germany). His research focus
 es on optimal control for PDEs\, model order reduction\, and data-driven
  modeling.\n\n\n\n📚 DDPS seminar is organized by libROM team ( www.libr
 om.net<http://www.librom.net> ).\n🗓️ DDPS seminar schedule: https://www
 .librom.net/ddps.html<https://www.youtube.com/redirect?event=video_descr
 iption&redir_token=QUFFLUhqblRkS256NDFhdjRyNHpRRFYwSm5jT0t0MVNvZ3xBQ3Jtc
 0treERKS1BuSkhPd3FHaXFqRDZPUnZtRjh1b01WcjJuczBSZFA1aGRranNtOVNFNlpONmxHS
