 ___________________________________________________\n\nFrom: Faculty <fa
 culty-bounces@cc.gatech.edu<mailto:faculty-bounces@cc.gatech.edu>> On Be
 half Of Sarkar\, Vivek\nSent: Wednesday\, February 19\, 2025 1:53 PM\nTo
 : Faculty@cc.gatech.edu<mailto:Faculty@cc.gatech.edu>\; Staff@cc.gatech.
 edu<mailto:Staff@cc.gatech.edu>\; phd-coc-official@cc.gatech.edu<mailto:
 phd-coc-official@cc.gatech.edu>\; ms-coc-official@cc.gatech.edu<mailto:m
 s-coc-official@cc.gatech.edu>\; cocadvisoryboard@cc.gatech.edu<mailto:co
 cadvisoryboard@cc.gatech.edu>\nSubject: [Faculty] Dean's Dispatch – new 
 committees for changing times\, kudos\n\n\n\nDear GT Computing community
 \,\n\n\n\nMany things are changing right now\, sometimes on a daily basi
 s.  I know this can be stressful for everyone.  As I mentioned during th
 e listening sessions\, our greatest strength is our people. We are lucky
  to have talented\, dedicated individuals who make ours a truly vibrant 
 community.  This is very much a time for all of us to come together and 
 express kindness and compassion for everyone\, especially those who are 
 the most vulnerable.  And be sure to extend kindness to yourself as well
 . No matter what the future brings\, we will face it together as a commu
 nity.\n\n\n\nTo keep communication channels active\, I am also schedulin
 g weekly virtual office hours with the Dean for all CoC faculty\, staff\
 , and students who would like to discuss any aspect of recent events and
  their potential impacts.  These will be held during 11:30am - 12:30pm o
 n Thursdays\, starting tomorrow (Feb 20th).  Please look out for a calen
 dar invitation and contact Cynthia Bryant (cebryant@cc.gatech.edu<mailto
 :cebryant@cc.gatech.edu>) if you don’t see one.\n\n\n\nIn addition to th
 e listening sessions\, I very much appreciated meeting with our college 
 advisory board members last Friday.  We are lucky to have such a talente
 d and experienced group of experts on our advisory board who care deeply
  about our college and are ready to share their advice and support in ev
 ery way that they can.  It was very helpful to hear their perspectives\,
  and I look forward to further discussions during our upcoming advisory 
 board meeting on April 3-4.\n\n\n\nIn other news\, I am pleased to annou
 nce that Melinda McDaniel is the new interim Associate Chair of the Scho
 ol of Computing Instruction. Melinda is a GT alumna and has a decade of 
 experience teaching our undergraduates --- we are lucky to have her in t
 his leadership position.  Many thanks to Melinda for stepping up this im
 portant role for SCI and the college!\n\n\n\nI'd also like to share upda
 tes on the two committees mentioned in last week's dispatch.  Both commi
 ttees will welcome getting input from all of you\, of course.\n\n\n\nCoC
  Contingency Planning Committee\n\nThis committee includes representatio
 n from faculty\, staff\, and students. Its scope will include staying ab
 reast of recent developments\, identifying scenarios that need contingen
 cy planning\, and recommending contingency efforts for those scenarios. 
  It includes the following members:\n\n  *   Greg Eisenhauer (Senior Res
 earch Scientist\, SCS)\n  *   Ada Gavrilovska (Professor\, SCS)\n  *   N
 eha Kumar (Associate Professor\, IC)\n  *   Kim Macleod (Associate Direc
 tor of Enterprise Services\, TSO)\n  *   Melinda McDaniel (Senior Lectur
 er\, SCI)\n  *   Peter Swire (J.Z. Liang Professor\, SCP and Scheller)\n
   *   Xiuwei Zhang (J.Z. Liang Early-Career Assistant Professor\, CSE)\n
 \n\n\nWe are still waiting on confirming the student member of this comm
 ittee and will announce their name when it is confirmed.\n\n\n\nCoC Task
  Force on New Strategies for Funding Computing Research\n\nThis task for
 ce will be in charge of thinking strategically about exploring new ways 
 to fund the research of CoC faculty\, especially in areas that are vulne
 rable to loss of funding. It includes the following members:\n\n  *   Ca
 rlos Batist (Assistant Director - Financial Operations\, SCS)\n  *   Tom
  Conte (Associate Dean for Research\, CoC\; and\, Professor\, SCS & ECE 
 - Task Force Lead)\n  *   Alberto Dainotti (Associate Professor\, SCS)\n
   *   Munmun De Choudhury (Associate Professor\, IC)\n  *   Rich DeMillo
  (Charlotte B. and Roger C. Warren Professor of Computing\, SCP)\n  *   
 Kristine Haskett (Executive Director Foundation Relations\, GT Office of
  Development)\n  *   Josiah Hester (Catherine M. and James E. Allchin Ea
 rly Career Associate Professor\, IC)\n  *   Tamara Pearson (Senior Direc
 tor\, Constellations Center\, CoC)\n  *   Chao Zhang (Assistant Professo
 r\, CSE)\n  *   Jason Zwang (Senior Director\, Development\, CoC)\n\n\n\
 nFinally\, I have kudos to share for members of our community\, as alway
 s:\n\n  *   Our student Competitive Programming team has dominated in re
 cent competitions.\n     *   Nearly 100 Tech students took part in the M
 eta Hacker Cup\, with six students making it to the third round. Ph.D. c
 andidate Sunghyeong Jo placed 8th in the third round and was one of 500 
 to make it to the final round\, where he placed 13th.\n     *   At the I
 nternational Collegiate Programming Contest (ICPC) regionals\, Georgia T
 ech teams secured four of the top 10 spots. The top Georgia Tech team of
  Weiyu Chen\, Kevin Shan\, and Xieting Chu solved all 13 problems\, plac
 ing second in the region and third nationally. Congratulations to all of
  the team members and to Abrahim Ladha (SCI)\, their faculty advisor. We
 ll done!\n  *   Our entrepreneurial capstone course has more than double
 d in size since we launched it in 2023\, with a current enrollment of 12
 6 students. In response\, the College is expanding its course sections a
 nd deepening our relationship with CREATE-X\, the Institute’s entreprene
 urship education initiative.  See the related story<https://nam12.safeli
 nks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cc.gatech.edu%2Fnews%2
 Fcollege-expands-partnership-create-x-double-down-entrepreneurship-curri
 culum&data=05%7C02%7Crzhang662%40gtvault.onmicrosoft.com%7Cbdf8e3d035794
 68bb2d708de237a61ec%7C482198bbae7b4b258b7a6d7f32faa083%7C1%7C0%7C6389872
 05779405245%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAu
 MDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sd
 ata=1EMjnBr1MXTwF5Aqe58sHyvDYl7eGzuNXmLY7kNa6ow%3D&reserved=0> for detai
 ls.\n\n\n\nThat’s all for this week.  Please stay warm!\n\n\n\nBest rega
 rds\,\n\n\n\nVivek\n\n\n\nPS -- I’d like to remind everyone that our ins
 titute’s Spring Compliance Training is under way\, and that everyone in 
 the college (including student employees) must complete it by February 2
 8. I have completed it myself and can attest that it’s not too cumbersom
 e … and it was interesting to see a module there related to GenAI!\n\n\n
 \nStudent programming team: https://www.cc.gatech.edu/news/competitive-p
