 lanl.gov";ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT:mailto:taitano@lanl.gov
CREATED:20260125T225044Z
DESCRIPTION:We will host Cecilia Pagliantini (Univerity of Pisa\, https:
 //numpi.dm.unipi.it/people/cecilia-pagliantini/) as a DDPS seminar speak
 er. Hope to see you many of you at the seminar:\n\n\nDynamical approxima
 tion and sensor placement for the state estimation of transport problems
 \n\nCecilia Pagliantini\n\nUniversity of Pisa\, Italy\n\nFriday\, April 
 11\, 2025\n10:00 AM\nWebEx\n\n\nAbstract: This talk focuses on the inver
 se problem of reconstructing an unknown function $u$ from a finite set o
 f measurements\, under the assumption that $u$ is the output of a parame
 tric differential equation with unknown input parameters. Typically\, th
 e target function $u$ belongs to an infinite-dimensional Hilbert space a
 nd the geometry of the solution set $M$ is not known a priori. One way t
 o reduce the complexity of the problem is to approximate $M$ by a linear
 \, finite dimensional subspace $V$ using model order reduction\, and to 
 search for an approximation $u^{\\star}\\in V$ to the state $u$. It is h
 owever known that such approach become ineffective for approximating the
  dynamics of transport problems. In this talk we will discuss how to add
 ress inverse problems for wave phenomena in the framework of Hamiltonian
  systems. The method combines symplectic dynamical low-rank approximatio
 n to update $V$ with dynamical placement of the sensors to ensure an acc
 urate reconstruction at all times. This talk is based on joint work with
  Olga Mula (TU/e) and Federico Vismara (TU/e).\n\nBio: Cecilia Paglianti
 ni is Assistant Professor in the Department of Mathematics at the Univer
 sity of Pisa\, Italy. After obtaining her PhD at the Seminar for Applied
  Mathematics of ETH Zurich\, Switzerland\, she held postdoctoral positio
 ns at the Los Alamos National Laboratory\, USA\, and at the EPFL\, Switz
 erland. She joined TU/e Eindhoven in 2020 as assistant professor in the 
 Centre for Analysis\, Scientific Computing and Applications (CASA) and m
 oved to the University of Pisa in 2023. Her research focuses on the deve
 lopment\, analysis and implementation of numerical methods for different
 ial equations. In particular\, she works on numerical discretizations an
 d model order reduction able to yield approximate models possessing the 
 same geometric structures and physical properties of the original models
 . She is also interested in robust numerical methods and efficient solve
 rs for fluid and kinetic plasma physics\, and for problems arising in co
 mputational electromagnetism.\n\n\n📚 DDPS seminar is organized by libRO
 M team ( www.librom.net<http://www.librom.net> ).\n🗓️ DDPS seminar sche
 dule: https://www.librom.net/ddps.html<https://www.youtube.com/redirect?
 event=video_description&redir_token=QUFFLUhqblRkS256NDFhdjRyNHpRRFYwSm5j
 T0t0MVNvZ3xBQ3Jtc0treERKS1BuSkhPd3FHaXFqRDZPUnZtRjh1b01WcjJuczBSZFA1aGRr
 anNtOVNFNlpONmxHSkRHLUZEeVA5Y24wVXRHRi1wdGdDT3E5RlFneHFVdUpNTk1KeEIwNjMz
 LTBTVjZueDRodXp0c2VLWTZDUQ&q=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.librom.net%2Fddps.html&v=
 uksMn36aecg>\n💻 LLNL News: https://www.llnl.gov/news<https://www.youtub
 e.com/redirect?event=video_description&redir_token=QUFFLUhqa2RhQlRlU3N0Q
 ThpOFhfeFJhclpSd0RRRkFGd3xBQ3Jtc0trdlZna1FQcmQtVG1CQ3VOUVoxekpPalR4ZlFUd
 jRXSmtJSEFmM3NacmJOSnA2M3dnYURFQU96WWc2VHIxNnhDYjRZWXRFc2NmRzBUR2hSd2ZmX
 1c0SXBtOXJDdFU2Tms2c1lZeEJHVkJ4X2FtUm5lTQ&q=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.llnl.gov%2
 Fnews&v=uksMn36aecg>\n📲 Instagram:  [cid:image001.png@01DB82B7.35BAEB30
 ]  / livermore_lab  <https://www.youtube.com/redirect?event=video_descri
 ption&redir_token=QUFFLUhqbjc2bndtcExQcG1SNnhGUUNBVm80bFF6M2xyZ3xBQ3Jtc0
 tsYUFYanBWT0Y5RnBseEtOckF2UnZrZG9jU1ZzeDlSZXZGTkpJXzJoU1NqeGJ4bVV5SzNCSU
