DESCRIPTION:We will start soon.\n\n--\nYoungsoo Choi\nhttps://people.lln
 l.gov/choi15\n• E-Mail: choi15@llnl.gov<mailto:choi15@llnl.gov>\n• Phone
 : (cell) 925-724-6834\n                (office) 925-422-0217\n\n\nFrom: 
 choi15@llnl.gov\nWhen: 10:00 AM - 11:00 AM January 24\, 2025\nSubject: [
 DDPS seminar] Is AI Shifting the Paradigm of Scientific Discovery?\nLoca
 tion: B453 R1012 Black Diamond Rm [Comp] (P-74)\; https://llnlfed.webex.
 com/llnlfed/j.php?MTID=me657090911f95690fa3b4b53da8de3b7\n\n\nWe will ho
 st Max Welling (University of Amsterdam\, https://staff.fnwi.uva.nl/m.we
 lling/) as a DDPS seminar speaker. Hope to see you many of you at the se
 minar:\n\n\nIs AI Shifting the Paradigm of Scientific Discovery?\n\nMax 
 Welling\n   [cid:image001.jpg@01DB6E46.BC5959E0]\nUniversity of Amsterda
 m\n\nFriday\, January 24\, 2025\n10:00 AM\nB453 R1012 & WebEx\n\n\nAbstr
 act: For a long time the most prominent application area of AI and ML wa
 s text and image data. However\, recently we have witnessed the rise of 
 a new application domain for AI: the natural sciences. With two recent N
 obel Prizes for AI in Physics and Chemistry we can confidently state tha
 t the field has established itself. In this talk I will review some of t
 he applications in science where AI has made a big impact. In some detai
 l I will discuss progress in ML Force Fields\, Classical DFT and PDE mod
 eling. I will also argue that mathematics\, physics and neuroscience con
 tinue to deliver inspiration for improving ML algorithms. I will end by 
 arguing that the field is not only intellectually interesting but we may
  indeed need this revolution to help us avert some of humanity's greates
 t challenges such as climate change.\n\nBio: Prof. Dr. Max Welling is a 
 full professor and research chair in machine learning at the University 
 of Amsterdam and a Merkin distinguished visiting professor at Caltech. H
 e is co-founder and CAIO of the startup CuspAI in Materials Design. He i
 s a fellow at the Canadian Institute for Advanced Research (CIFAR) and t
 he European Lab for Learning and Intelligent Systems (ELLIS) where he se
 rved on the founding board. His previous appointments include Partner an
 d VP at Microsoft Research\, VP at Qualcomm Technologies\, professor at 
 UC Irvine.\n\nHe finished his PhD in theoretical high energy physics und
 er supervision of Nobel laureate prof. Gerard ‘t Hooft. He then switched
  fields to focus on machine learning\, first as a postdoc at Caltech und
 er supervision of prof. Pietro Perona and then as postdoc under supervis
 ion of Nobel laureate prof. Geoffrey Hinton at UCL & U. Toronto.\n\nMax 
 Welling has served as associate editor in chief of IEEE TPAMI from 2011-
 2015\, he serves on the advisory board of the Neurips foundation since 2
 015\, he is co-founder of the European Lab for Learning and Intelligence
  Systems (ELLIS) and served on its board until 2021\, he has been progra
 m chair and general chair of Neurips in 2013 and 2014 respectively. He w
 as also program chair of AISTATS in 2009 and ECCV in 2016 and general ch
 air and co-founder of MIDL 2018. Max Welling is recipient of the ECCV Ko
 enderink Prize in 2010\, and the 10 year Test of Time awards at ICML in 
 2021 and ICLR in 2024.\n\n\n\n📚 DDPS seminar is organized by libROM tea
 m ( www.librom.net<http://www.librom.net> ).\n🗓️ DDPS seminar schedule:
  https://www.librom.net/ddps.html<https://www.youtube.com/redirect?event
