 hy \; Khairallah\, Saad A. \; Schildbach\, Marcus \; Anand\, Nagamangala
  <nkanand@tamu.edu>\; Syal\, Megan Bruck \; Mcnenly\, Matthew J. \; Gard
 ner\, Daniel \; Kushwaha\, Shashank <kushwaha1@llnl.gov>\; Nataraj\, Chi
 raag M <cmnatar@sandia.gov>\; Koning\, Joe \; Wood\, Brandon\nSubject: [
 DDPS seminar] Is AI Shifting the Paradigm of Scientific Discovery?\nWhen
 : Friday\, January 24\, 2025 6:00 PM-7:00 PM.\nWhere: B453 R1012 Black D
 iamond Rm [Comp] (P-74)\; https://llnlfed.webex.com/llnlfed/j.php?MTID=m
 e657090911f95690fa3b4b53da8de3b7\n\nWe will host Max Welling (University
  of Amsterdam\, https://staff.fnwi.uva.nl/m.welling/) as a DDPS seminar 
 speaker. Hope to see you many of you at the seminar:\n\n\nIs AI Shifting
  the Paradigm of Scientific Discovery?\n\nMax Welling\n   [cid:image001.
 jpg@01DB63A6.67622D90]\nUniversity of Amsterdam\n\nFriday\, January 24\,
  2025\n10:00 AM\nB453 R1012 & WebEx\n\n\nAbstract: For a long time the m
 ost prominent application area of AI and ML was text and image data. How
 ever\, recently we have witnessed the rise of a new application domain f
 or AI: the natural sciences. With two recent Nobel Prizes for AI in Phys
 ics and Chemistry we can confidently state that the field has establishe
 d itself. In this talk I will review some of the applications in science
  where AI has made a big impact. In some detail I will discuss progress 
 in ML Force Fields\, Classical DFT and PDE modeling. I will also argue t
 hat mathematics\, physics and neuroscience continue to deliver inspirati
 on for improving ML algorithms. I will end by arguing that the field is 
 not only intellectually interesting but we may indeed need this revoluti
 on to help us avert some of humanity's greatest challenges such as clima
 te change.\n\nBio: Prof. Dr. Max Welling is a full professor and researc
 h chair in machine learning at the University of Amsterdam and a Merkin 
 distinguished visiting professor at Caltech. He is co-founder and CAIO o
 f the startup CuspAI in Materials Design. He is a fellow at the Canadian
  Institute for Advanced Research (CIFAR) and the European Lab for Learni
 ng and Intelligent Systems (ELLIS) where he served on the founding board
 . His previous appointments include Partner and VP at Microsoft Research
 \, VP at Qualcomm Technologies\, professor at UC Irvine.\n\nHe finished 
 his PhD in theoretical high energy physics under supervision of Nobel la
 ureate prof. Gerard ‘t Hooft. He then switched fields to focus on machin
 e learning\, first as a postdoc at Caltech under supervision of prof. Pi
 etro Perona and then as postdoc under supervision of Nobel laureate prof
 . Geoffrey Hinton at UCL & U. Toronto.\n\nMax Welling has served as asso
 ciate editor in chief of IEEE TPAMI from 2011-2015\, he serves on the ad
 visory board of the Neurips foundation since 2015\, he is co-founder of 
 the European Lab for Learning and Intelligence Systems (ELLIS) and serve
 d on its board until 2021\, he has been program chair and general chair 
 of Neurips in 2013 and 2014 respectively. He was also program chair of A
 ISTATS in 2009 and ECCV in 2016 and general chair and co-founder of MIDL
  2018. Max Welling is recipient of the ECCV Koenderink Prize in 2010\, a
 nd the 10 year Test of Time awards at ICML in 2021 and ICLR in 2024.\n\n
